  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through Sunday)\nIssued at 1226 AM CDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nOn Tuesday, an upper level ridge will be centered across the \nSouthern Plains. This will result in hot and dry conditions across \nthe area. Highs will be in the mid 90s to near 102 with overnight \nlows in the 70s to near 80.\n\nAs we head into Wednesday, an upper level ridge will become centered \nacross the Southwest United States, with another upper level ridge \ncentered across the Southeast United States. Thursday into the \nweekend, the Southeast U.S. ridge will weaken while the ridge to our \nwest strengthens. This will result in a continuation of the hot and \ngenerally dry conditions. The one caveat is that both the GFS and \nECMWF are indicating a cold front into Oklahoma and north Texas late \nWednesday into Thursday. If this front can track a bit farther \nsouth, this could bring a chance of showers and thunderstorms to the \narea, especially across our northern counties. For now, have kept \nrain chances between 15 and 20 percent across the Big Country as \nconfidence remains low. Above normal temperatures will continue \nthrough this time frame with highs in the mid 90s to near 104 and \novernight lows in the 70s.",
